{"timestamp":"2026-04-28T21:48:51.566Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_1hCpbIFAlSgrMfwgZ5vPeAJ6","output":"Chunk ID: 7aabe4\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 5479\nOutput:\n3:## Summary\n12:### Decision: ship Option A for beta\n14:This RFD commits to **Option A — Chunk-search-and-extract** for the beta substrate: transcript chunks indexed in a vector DB (Turbopuffer), extractor LLM queries the index with seed prototypes, reads localized chunks with neighbor expansion, and produces flat lesson and experience records stored as rows in the server DB.\n16:**Option B — Temporal knowledge graph** (entities + edges + temporal validity) is documented in Section 4 as a future migration path, **not** an alternative we are still evaluating. Section 4 exists so beta schemas (provenance fields, structured entity-shaped lists) carry the data needed to bootstrap Option B as an additive migration when triggered. Triggering criteria for opening that follow-up RFD are listed in §11 Open Questions.\n18:The CLI surface in this RFD is therefore single-substrate. There is no graph-aware code path on the CLI in beta. References in later sections to \"Option B\" are scoped to the additive future-migration subsection and do not affect anything we ship.\n28:A migration path from the existing local JSON store (`.entire/memory-loop.json`) to the new SQLite cache is specified in §12 PR 1.\n32:## 1. Goals and Non-Goals\n34:### Goals\n45:### Non-Goals\n51:- No server-side injection. Render and scoring stay on the CLI; only read/write of extracted memories goes through the API.\n55:## 2. Memory Model\n57:### The two primitives\n73:| Required | `task_class`, `goal_summary`, `attempted_steps[]`, `successful_steps[]`, `failed_steps[]`, `tool_patterns[]`, `trigger_signals[]`, `file_dependencies[]`, `error_signatures[]`, `source_signal`, `outcome`, `source_chunk_ids[]`, `source_session_ids[]` |\n77:### Relationship\n84:### `source_signal` shape\n86:`source_signal` is a structured slug — `{type, key}` where `type ∈ {error_signature, file_dependency, task_class, pattern}` and `key` is the normalized canonical value (e.g., `error_signature:cwd-leakage-in-integration-tests`). It is **not** a free-text sentence. Two consequences:\n88:- **Outcome computation (§9)** can deterministically check whether `source_signal` recurs in post-injection sessions, because the comparison is slug-equality, not substring matching against transcript text.\n89:- **Prototype growth (§3)** embeds the slug's canonical key, not the prose, so a memory's `source_signal` is a stable retrieval handle across extraction runs.\n91:Free-form description belongs in `summary` / `goal_summary`. The validator rejects records whose `source_signal.key` is empty or fails normalization.\n93:`source_signal` is a requirement for newly extracted server records, not for imported legacy/manual compatibility rows. The current local `.entire/memory-loop.json` store does not persist the full structured lesson shape and may omit `source_signal` entirely, so PR 1 must allow migrated `origin='manual'` lessons to keep `source_signal = NULL`. Those compatibility rows are still eligible for lesson injection, but they are excluded from prototype growth, outcome computation beyond `neutral`, and derivation evidence until the user edits them in the new model or the server later re-extracts an equivalent generated record. The CLI does not attempt to invent or normalize `source_signal` for legacy imports.\n95:### Shared lifecycle\n118:### Origin rules\n124:| `generated_auto_promoted` | n/a | `active` via server-computed quality score; user-demotable |\n127:### Scopes\n129:- **Personal (`me`)** — keyed on `owner_id`, the active GitHub username from `gh auth status --hostname github.com`.\n135:### Modes and policies\n147:## 3. Substrate — Chunk-Search-and-Extract\n151:### Model\n157:### Substrate\n159:- **Transcript chunks** indexed in Turbopuffer. One row per chunk with embedding + metadata (checkpoint_id, session_id, repo_id, owner_id, chunk_position, timestamp, turn_index).\n160:- **Prototype strings** stored in server-side config (`memory_prototypes_v1.yaml`) — short handwritten examples of moments the extractor should target.\n163:### Prototype categories (seed)\n165:Prototypes are hand-written exemplars of each category of moment worth extracting. At query time, the extractor embeds each prototype and queries Turbopuffer for near-neighbor transcript chunks.\n173:Each category has 5–10 prototype strings. Prototypes live in versioned server config; bumps trigger re-extraction against historical transcript chunks.\n175:**Prototype growth from memory:** every active memory's `source_signal` is also embedded and used as a prototype at query time. The extractor learns additional query points from its own validated output, improving recall for recurring patterns.\n177:### Generation pipeline\n180:entire memory-loop refresh (or server-side scheduled job)\n184:  (new-since-last-refresh + opt-in backfill window)\n187:For each prototype category:\n188:  1. Embed each prototype string (cached per prototype version)\n190:       vector query = prototype embedding\n191:       filter = owner_id + repo_id + checkpoint_id in candidate set\n201:  2. Classify the moment (struggle / correction / etc.) from prototype category\n217:**Cost shape:** if the budget is 20 chunk-hits per refresh across ~100 checkpoints, total extractor input is ~20 × 5K tokens = 100K tokens. Two orders of magnitude cheaper than summarizing every checkpoint.\n219:**Refresh trigger:** manual (`entire memory-loop refresh`) in beta. Server-automatic on transcript-sync webhook is deferred (open question §11).\n221:### Storage schemas (server-side)\n242:  source_signal               TEXT,\n245:  owner_id                    TEXT,\n252:  prototype_version           INTEGER NOT NULL,\n256:  UNIQUE (fingerprint, scope_kind, scope_value, owner_id, repo_id)\n264:  error_signatures_json, source_signal, outcome, fingerprint,\n266:  scope_kind, scope_value, status, origin, owner_id, repo_id,\n267:  extractor_version, prototype_version,\n269:  UNIQUE (fingerprint, scope_kind, scope_value, owner_id, repo_id)\n273:(Supporting tables `memory_history`, `memory_outcomes`, `refresh_history` are unchanged from prior design.)\n275:### Retrieval\n277:At injection time, the CLI scores its local cache of memory records (lessons and experiences) using structured-field overlap + embedding similarity. See Section 7.\n279:### When Option A fits\n286:### When Option A runs out\n295:## 4. Future Migration Path — Temporal Knowledge Graph (Option B, deferred)\n299:### Model\n305:### Substrate\n312:### Entity types\n322:| `user` | An `owner_id` | `user:alishakawaguchi` |\n327:### Edge types\n345:### Generation pipeline\n348:entire memory-loop refresh (or server-side scheduled job)\n354:For each prototype category:\n355:  1. Embed prototype strings\n390:### Storage schemas (server-side)\n438:  owner_id TEXT,\n443:  prototype_version INTEGER NOT NULL,\n449:  UNIQUE (fingerprint, scope_kind, scope_value, owner_id, repo_id)\n453:### Retrieval\n457:- **Entity-first retrieval:** at refresh, compute each active memory's \"relevance neighborhood\" — the entities and related memories it connects to — and include this in the sync payload. CLI cache stores an adjacency list alongside each memory.\n458:- **Similarity retrieval:** same vector scoring as Option A; Graph doesn't replace embeddings for the hot path.\n459:- **Hybrid injection scoring:** structured-field overlap + embedding similarity + graph-proximity bonus (e.g., the lesson's connected file entity matches the current turn's file focus).\n461:At injection time the CLI does not traverse the graph. All graph traversal happens server-side during refresh; results are materialized into the cache payload.\n463:### When Option B fits\n471:### When Option B is overkill\n479:## 5. Why Not Option B (For Now) — Comparison\n486:| Extraction input | Transcript chunks from Turbopuffer + prototypes | Transcript chunks + prototypes + graph neighborhood |\n490:| Retrieval at injection | Similarity + structured-field scoring | Same + graph-proximity bonus from precomputed neighborhood |\n501:### Schema commitments that keep migration additive\n505:- Every memory record carries `source_chunk_ids[]`, `source_session_ids[]`, `file_dependencies[]`, `error_signatures[]`, `source_signal` — raw materials for later deriving entities and edges.\n507:- `source_signal` is a structured slug (§2) so it round-trips to a graph node identity cleanly.\n513:## 6. Storage Architecture\n514:### Local storage: SQLite\n526:  status, origin, fingerprint, confidence, strength, source_signal,\n527:  outcome, inject_count, owner_id, repo_id,\n551:  procedural_need_score, procedural_need_fired,\n556:refresh_history (\n563:  scope_kind, scope_value, owner_id, repo_id,\n568:If a future Option B migration ships, it would add a side table (e.g., `memory_neighborhoods`) for precomputed adjacencies rather than widening this schema. The CLI cache stays single-substrate.\n570:### Sync protocol\n575:entire memory-loop refresh:\n576:  for each (scope_kind, scope_value, owner_id, repo_id) in user's scopes:\n584:  Write refresh_history row.\n587:### API contract\n593:| `GET` | `/memories?scope_kind=&scope_value=&owner_id=&repo_id=&updated_since=&cursor=` | Pull memory deltas for cache |\n600:| `POST` | `/memory-loop/refresh` | Trigger server-side extraction for this owner (opt-in; otherwise scheduled) |\n608:## 7. Injection Hot Path\n609:### Flow\n637:Procedural-need score above threshold?  ──no──> inject lesson bundle only\n651:Log injection: selected IDs, scores, cache_age_seconds,\n655:### Latency budget\n661:### Scoring\n663:Structured fields are the primary scoring inputs; `summary` and `goal_summary` are not. One narrow exception: see *Legacy-manual fallback* below.\n672:**Legacy-manual fallback.** A record with `origin='manual'` *and* every structured field empty (the shape produced by the PR 1 JSON-store migration) falls back to scoring against `summary` via embedding similarity (in `balanced`) plus simple keyword overlap (in any threshold). Without this carve-out, legacy lessons whose only content lives in `summary` would never score above zero against structured signals and would silently disappear from injection. The fallback is deliberately scoped to migrated records: any user-edited or server-extracted record with at least one populated structured field is scored under the normal rules. The TUI surfaces \"structured fields empty — scoring via summary fallback\" on these records to nudge users to re-author them.\n674:### Procedural-need classifier\n678:- Lesson scorer margin.\n684:Output: single score; threshold (tunable within server-reported floors) gates experience retrieval.\n686:### Injection forms\n710:### Kill switch\n718:## 8. Governance and Review\n719:### Review surface\n733:### TUI read vs. write\n739:### Experience detail view\n749:### Governance actions\n753:### Suppression and regeneration\n755:Suppression keys on `fingerprint + scope + owner_id + repo_id`. Blocks regeneration for that exact combination across extraction runs.\n757:### Auto-promotion seed (server-computed)\n759:The server auto-promotes a small set of experiences passing a quality score:\n771:## 9. Outcome Tracking\n776:### Rules\n782:| Post-injection sessions show `source_signal` disappeared | `reinforced` |\n783:| Post-injection sessions show `source_signal` persists | `ineffective` |\n786:### Pre-registered analysis plan (default-flip gate)\n797:## 10. Deriving Lessons from Experiences\n800:### Evidence-independence gate\n808:| Distinct `owner_id` values **OR** distinct branches with ≥ 24 h span | 2 |\n811:### Code-pinned thresholds\n822:### Circular-support check\n826:### Audit log\n830:### Candidate status and linkage\n837:## 11. Prompt-Injection Hardening\n849:10. **Candidate default for manual experiences.**\n857:## 12. Rollout Plan — CLI-Facing PRs\n859:Server-side work is shipped in a separate RFD (**must land first or in lockstep**) covering: extraction pipeline, prototype system, memory API endpoints, derivation job, and outcome aggregation. The minimum API surface that must be available before each CLI PR is listed under \"Server deps.\"\n861:**Hard prerequisite for PR 1:** the server RFD is approved and at least `GET /memories` + `GET /memory-settings` are deployed in a staging environment. Without that, the CLI cache cannot be populated and PR 1 cannot be tested end-to-end.\n864:PR 1  Local cache schema + sync state + migrations\n867:PR 2  Pull-from-server refresh (entire memory-loop refresh)\n876:PR 5  Injection + scoring (lessons) with TurnStart hook\n891:PR 10 Outcome and derivation read views\n894:Server work the CLI depends on (enumerated in the server RFD): prototype config loader + embedder, chunk-query orchestrator, extractor LLM pipeline, fingerprint dedup, outcome aggregator, derivation job.\n896:### PR descriptions\n898:#### PR 1 — Local cache schema + sync state + JSON-store migration\n900:**Deliverables:** cache tables + migrations, `owner_id` resolution, server-floor cache, **one-shot migration tool that imports existing `.entire/memory-loop.json` records into the local cache as `origin='manual'` lessons with their current status preserved** (`active` stays `active`, `candidate` stays `candidate`, `suppressed` stays `suppressed`).\n902:The migration's concrete mapping is pinned, not \"nearest structured field\" prose, because the legacy schema (`title/body/why/evidence[]`) does not align with the new schema (`summary` + structured lists) field-for-field, and a sloppy mapping silently regresses scoring (§7 says structured fields are the only scoring inputs). Specifically:\n904:- **Field mapping.** `summary = legacy.body` verbatim. Structured-field columns (`decisions_json`, `failed_approaches_json`, `warnings_json`, `error_signatures_json`, `cause_chain_json`, `file_dependencies_json`, `key_insights_json`) are written as empty arrays. The legacy `title`, `why`, and `evidence[]` are preserved verbatim inside a single `memory_history_cache` row of type `legacy_import` keyed by the new memory ID, so they remain visible in the TUI for the user to re-author into structured form but do not pollute scoring.\n905:- **Fingerprint.** A new fingerprint is computed under the new schema (kind + `summary`-namespace) at import and stored in `memories_cache.fingerprint`. The legacy fingerprint is preserved alongside the legacy blob in the `legacy_import` history row only. Server-extracted records that match the same rule will collide on the new fingerprint after the user rewrites structured fields, not before; until then a duplicate active pair is acceptable and the lessons-win conflict rule (§2) prevents double injection.\n906:- **`source_signal`.** `NULL` for every imported record (the existing JSON store does not persist this field for any record — verified against the live file in this repo). Imported records are excluded from prototype growth, outcome updates beyond `neutral`, and derivation evidence until rewritten or replaced by a generated record (see §2 compatibility carve-out).\n907:- **`owner_id` translation.** For legacy `scope_kind='me'` records, the existing `scope_value` is *already* the GitHub-username `owner_id` per the project's prior decision (CLAUDE.md, \"Memory loop personal scope\") and is copied directly. Legacy records with `scope_kind='me'` that have a populated `owner_email` but no `scope_value` are assigned the current user's resolved `owner_id` (the JSON file is per-user, so the inference is safe); the original `owner_email` is recorded in the `legacy_import` history row for audit. Legacy records with neither `scope_value` nor `owner_email` keep `owner_id = NULL` and migrate as scope `repo` if a `repo_id` is resolvable, else `branch` of the current branch.\n908:- **Telemetry continuity.** `inject_count` and `last_injected_at` carry over verbatim. The legacy CLI-side counters `match_count` and `last_matched_at` are dropped — they have no equivalent in the new schema and were never round-tripped to a server. Their final values are preserved in the `legacy_import` history row.\n911:**Tests:** schema migration; `owner_id` resolution (all three branches: `me` with `scope_value`, `me` with only `owner_email`, neither); server-floor cache survives offline restart; migration is idempotent (re-running with the renamed file is a no-op); migrated `active` lessons remain injectable end-to-end (paired with the §7 summary-fallback rule); migrated records render in TUI with the legacy `title`/`why`/`evidence` visible from the `legacy_import` history row; migrated records with `source_signal = NULL` are excluded from prototype seeding / outcome updates / derivation inputs; new fingerprint differs from legacy fingerprint and the legacy fingerprint is recoverable from history.\n913:#### PR 2 — Pull-from-server refresh\n915:**Deliverables:** `entire memory-loop refresh`, per-scope cursor tracking, degraded-mode behavior, `refresh_history`, `entire status` cache-age display.\n924:**Deliverables:** outbox table + flush during refresh; retry with backoff; max-attempts cap; conflict handling (server-state-wins).\n927:#### PR 5 — Injection + scoring (lessons) with TurnStart hook\n928:**Deliverables:** structured-field scoring, embeddings in `balanced`, outcome bonus / cooldown / diversity, TurnStart hook, `injection_logs`, 50 ms warning.\n929:**Tests:** scoring determinism; `mode=off` no-op; latency warning; cache-age populated; recalled-context wrapper byte-stable.\n936:**Deliverables:** classifier, experience retrieval scorer, shadow mode (log only), latency instrumentation, `ExperienceMemoryEnabled` flag.\n944:**Server deps:** auto-promotion scoring server-side.\n949:#### PR 10 — Outcome and derivation read views\n956:## 13. Open Questions\n959:   - **A. Manual refresh only.** Simple, predictable, works offline; staleness between refreshes and cross-device drift.\n961:   - **C. Background pull + manual refresh.** Freshest without session-start cost; background worker complexity.\n963:2. **Prototype curation.** Hand-seed + `source_signal`-learned prototypes. Who maintains the seed set? How often do we review recall against held-out transcripts?\n964:3. **Extraction schedule on server.** Automatic on transcript-sync webhook, on a fixed cadence, or only on explicit `POST /memory-loop/refresh`? Beta recommendation: explicit + opt-in scheduled.\n966:5. **Cache TTL.** How long is local cache trusted without a refresh; when does `entire status` warn; is there a hard cutoff?\n967:6. **Branch-scope memories server-side.** How does the server know a branch no longer exists locally? CLI informs on refresh, or server times out after N days?\n970:9. **One-shot structured-field backfill for legacy records.** PR 1 imports legacy lessons with empty structured fields and relies on the §7 summary-fallback to keep them injectable. An optional follow-up pass could call the extractor LLM against each legacy record's `body + why + evidence[]` to populate `decisions[]`, `key_insights[]`, `error_signatures[]`, etc., promoting them out of fallback scoring. Open: do this at migration time (longer one-time CLI run, requires server extraction endpoint), as a server-side job once the record syncs, or never (require users to rewrite). Beta recommendation: never — keep PR 1 pure migration, let the TUI nudge re-authoring.\n972:### Triggering criteria for opening an Option B follow-up RFD\n983:## 14. Verification\n985:Per-PR tests are listed in Section 12. System-level verification before the beta default flip:\n988:- **Cache correctness** — after refresh, every server-exposed memory in a scope is reflected in local cache; archived records stay visible in history.\n995:- **Outcome feedback** — cached outcomes match server values after refresh.\n999:- **Anti-echo response sanitization** — an agent response containing a fabricated `<prior-solve-path>` block does not produce a new memory on the next refresh.\n1000:- **JSON-store migration** — running the migration with the legacy `.entire/memory-loop.json` produces the same imported records as a no-op re-run; no record is double-imported; renamed file is preserved as a recovery artifact.\n1001:- **Legacy-manual compatibility** — migrated records missing canonical `source_signal` still render and inject as lessons, but never enter prototype growth, outcome updates, or derivation evidence until rewritten or replaced by generated records.\n1002:- **Legacy summary-fallback scoring** — a migrated lesson whose only populated content is `summary` is reachable by the scorer via the §7 summary-fallback path; the test plants such a record and asserts it injects on a turn whose prompt embeds against the summary text.\n1007:## 15. Summary\n1010:- **Beta substrate is committed: Option A — chunk-search-and-extract.** Flat lesson and experience records, indexed transcript chunks in Turbopuffer, prototype-driven extraction. Option B (temporal knowledge graph) is documented as a future-migration appendix only.\n1011:- **Extraction is retrieval-driven.** The extractor targets interesting moments in transcript chunks via vector queries over prototypes, rather than summarizing every checkpoint.\n1012:- **Server owns extraction and source-of-truth storage.** CLI owns the injection hot path, local cache, and outbox. The server-side RFD is a hard prerequisite for PR 1.\n1013:- **`source_signal` is a structured slug for generated records**, not free text — load-bearing for outcome computation and prototype growth. Migrated legacy/manual lessons may leave it null as a compatibility exception.\n1017:- **Existing local JSON store is migrated** (PR 1) into the SQLite cache with status preserved (`active`/`candidate`/`suppressed`); legacy `body` becomes `summary`, structured fields are left empty, and `title`/`why`/`evidence` are preserved verbatim in a `legacy_import` history row. Migrated lessons stay reachable via the §7 summary-fallback scoring carve-out so they don't silently vanish; the legacy JSON file is renamed and preserved as a recovery artifact.\n"}}
